The source language is dropped rather than written to a second file (e.g. <name>.ja.gpt.srt): the user chose to keep Whisper's transcript as the only native subtitles, and a gpt rewrite of the same language had no use yet. The shared stages now always run first, as they did with no targets, because a detected language is only known after transcription; the per-language passes then only ever start at translate, so --force uses from_stage='translate' for every language instead of forcing the whole first pass. When --from is given, the language is dropped up front so a source-only --to needs no API key; with detection the key check still runs, since the targets aren't known to be redundant until transcription.
